<aside name="linearApproximationFormula"><title>Formula - Linear approximation using the tangent line:</title>
  <md>
  <mrow>y \amp= f(a) +f'(a)(x-a)</mrow>
  </md>
  <p>where, 
    <ul>
      <li><m>a</m> is the input value at which the tangent line is to be evaluated and </li>
      <li><m>y</m> is an approximation to <m>f(x)</m> for input values of <m>x</m> near <m>a</m>.</li>
    </ul>
  </p>
</aside>

  

<problem><title>Video review questions</title>
<setup>

</setup>

<exercise includeAutoNumber="true"><title />
<p>Select the option that best completes the sentence.</p>
<p>The tangent line, or the linear approximation, to a function at a point <m>a</m>
  <answer name="ans1a">
    <choice> is negative when the rate of change of the function <m>f</m> is greater than 0 at <m>a</m></choice>
    <choice credit="1"> is a good approximation to the function <m>f</m> at points near <m>a</m></choice>
    <choice> has a slope that is always equal to the value of the function <m>f</m> at <m>a</m></choice>
  </answer>
  <feedback condition="$ans1a = $_choice1.text">Actually, the opposite of this statement is true: if the rate of change of the function is <em>greater</em> than 0, then the derivative will have a <em>positive</em> value at <m>a</m>.</feedback>
  <feedback condition="$ans1a = $_choice3.text">This statement is not generally true - the value of a function is <em>not</em> necessarily equal to it's derivative (or the slope of its tangent line) at every <m>x</m> (or any arbitrary point <m>a</m>). But, there is a special function where this is, in fact, the case: <m>f(x) = e^x</m>.
  </feedback>
  <hint>See <ref to="$vid_section">Key Moment 1</ref></hint>
</p>
</exercise>

<exercise includeAutoNumber="true"><title />
<p>Given a function <m>f(x) = x^2</m>, let's determine the equation of the tangent line to <m>f(x)</m> at <m>x = 3</m>:
  <ul>
    <li><p>First, what is <m>f'(x)</m> = 
      <answer name="ans2a">2x</answer>   
    </p></li>
    
    <feedback condition="$ans2a.nSubmissions > 2 and $ans2a.creditAchieved !=1">Here, we need to use the <ref to="doenet:7KNaZhtcLkYEubRxQt2Ak4">power rule</ref> to differentiate <m>f(x) = x^2</m></feedback>
   
    <li><p>Note that in this question, the <m>a</m> in the linear approximation formula given <ref to="$linearApproximationFormula">here</ref> is:  <m>a = </m>
      <answer inline="true" name="ans2a_a">3</answer>
      <feedback condition="$ans2a_a.nSubmissions = 2">Check the formula <ref to="$linearApproximationFormula">here</ref>. In this formula, <m>a</m> represents the input value at which we are calculating the tangent line. In this case, we are determining the equation of the tangent line at x = ....?</feedback>
      So, then <m>f'(a) = </m>
      <answer name="ans2b">6</answer>
    </p></li>
    <feedback condition="$ans2b.nSubmissions = 2">Using the expression you calculated for <m>f'(x)</m> (it should be a function involving <m>x</m>...), substitute the value you found for <m>a</m> from the previous question.</feedback>  
    
    <li><p>Finally, <m>f(a) = </m>
      <answer name="ans2c">9</answer>
    </p></li>
    <feedback condition="$ans2c.nSubmissions = 2">Using the expression you calculated for <m>f'(x)</m> (it should be a function involving <m>x</m>...), substitute the value you found for <m>a</m> from the previous question.</feedback>  
    <li><p>We now have everything we need to write the equation for the tangent line to <m>f</m> at <m>a</m>. It is:
  
      <choiceInput name="choice2d">
        <choice><m>y = </m><math>$ans2c - $ans2a ( $ans2a_a + x)</math></choice>
        <choice><m>y = </m><math>$ans2c + $ans2a_a ( $ans2b - x)</math></choice>
        <choice credit="1"><m>y = </m><math>$ans2c + $ans2b (x - $ans2a_a)</math></choice>
      </choiceInput>
      <answer name="ans2d">
        <award>
          <when>$choice2d.selectedIndex = 3 and $ans2a.creditAchieved = 1 and $ans2a_a.creditAchieved = 1 and $ans2b.creditAchieved = 1 and $ans2c.creditAchieved = 1
          </when>
        </award> 
      </answer>
      <feedback condition="$ans2d.nSubmissions > 0 and $ans2d.creditAchieved != 1"> Note, in this part you need to get all the previous responses to question 2 correct in order to obtain the final correct equation.</feedback>
    </p></li>
  </ul>
</p>
</exercise>
<hint>See <ref to="$vid_section">Key Moment 2</ref> for a similar example..(with a trickier derivative)</hint>

</problem>

</section>